.TH MCRON 1 "2026-10-12" "1.1.0" "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
mcron \- minimal cron daemon with catch-up of missed jobs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mcron
[\fB\-f\fR | \fB\-v\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mcron
reads job schedules from a flat configuration file and runs the commands
with
.BR /bin/sh (1)
at the scheduled minute. If the system was off or suspended, jobs whose
schedule fell into the gap are run once after it comes back
(anacron-like catch-up).
.PP
By default
.B mcron
detaches and runs in the background. Output of started jobs is discarded.
Start-ups of jobs and configuration problems are logged to
.BR syslog (3)
with facility
.BR cron .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Stay in the foreground and also log to standard error. Use this when
.B mcron
is supervised, for example by runit.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is
.IR /etc/mcron.conf .
Each line holds five schedule fields followed by a command:
.PP
.RS
.I min hour dom mon dow command
.RE
.TP
.B min
Minute, 0\-59
.TP
.B hour
Hour, 0\-23
.TP
.B dom
Day of the month, 1\-31
.TP
.B mon
Month, 1\-12
.TP
.B dow
Day of the week, 0\-7 (0 and 7 are Sunday)
.PP
Every field is either
.B *
(any value) or a single number. Lists, ranges and steps such as
.BR 1,15 ,
.B 1\-5
or
.B */5
are not supported; a line containing them is rejected and reported to
syslog. If both
.B dom
and
.B dow
are given, both must match (this differs from POSIX cron). Lines starting
with
.B #
and empty lines are ignored. Lines longer than 254 characters are ignored.
Times are interpreted in the local time zone.
.PP
The file must be a regular file owned by the user running
.B mcron
and must not be writable by group or others, otherwise it is not used.
Changes are picked up automatically.
.SH CATCH-UP
.B mcron
records the last minute it processed in
.IR /var/lib/mcron/last_tick .
After a gap of up to 32 days every job whose schedule matched any minute
in the gap is run once, regardless of how many runs were missed. For a
longer gap, or on the first start, only the current minute is processed.
If the clock is set back, nothing is run until time passes the recorded
minute again. Jobs that run longer than their interval may overlap; use
.BR flock (1)
inside the command to prevent that.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mcron.conf
Configuration file.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mcron/
State directory. Must be owned by the user running
.B mcron
and not writable by group or others.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mcron/last_tick
Last processed minute, as seconds since the Epoch.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mcron/mcron.pid
Pid file, locked while the daemon runs. A second instance refuses to start.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B mcron
exits with a non-zero status if it cannot set up its state directory, is
already running, or is called with invalid arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cron (8),
.BR flock (1),
.BR syslog (3)
.SH AUTHOR
